(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-2-pd1-exam2-master-exam-form-c"]={"title":"PD1 Exam 2 Comprehensive Master Exam — Form C","category":"Physical Diagnosis","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Egophony is assessed by having the patient say a prolonged 'E'; a positive finding is when the sound heard through the stethoscope changes to...","choices":["an 'A' sound (a nasal, goat-bleating quality)","exactly the same clear, unchanged 'E' sound","a clearly whispered 'ninety-nine' phrase instead","complete silence with no audible sound at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"In egophony, the 'E' sound the patient says is transmitted through the stethoscope as an 'A' sound with a nasal, bleating quality — classically heard over an area of consolidation or along the upper border of a pleural effusion. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 52"},{"q":"When taking the skin/hair/nail history, common or concerning symptoms to screen for include hair loss, rashes, growths, sores, lumps/bumps, dryness, itching, and...","choices":["increased urinary frequency, especially at night","new-onset chest pain during exertion","changes (for example, in moles or existing lesions)","new-onset intermittent visual disturbances"],"answer":2,"exp":"The symptom checklist includes hair loss, rashes, growths, sores, lumps/bumps, dryness, itching, and any changes noted by the patient, such as changes in existing moles or lesions. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"Color vision is screened using pseudoisochromatic color plates; if color vision is intact, the patient should be able to...","choices":["see absolutely nothing distinguishable within the color plate","identify the embedded figure, usually a number, within the plate","correctly read a standard line on a Snellen visual acuity eye chart","identify only simple geometric shapes, never any embedded numbers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Pseudoisochromatic plates embed a figure (typically a number) using dots of subtly different colors; a patient with intact color vision can identify the figure, while a patient with a color vision deficiency may not. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 21"},{"q":"Factors that can cause a falsely inaccurate oral temperature reading include mouth breathing, smoking, drinking hot/cold liquids, and...","choices":["only the type of thermometer used","nothing else affects oral temperature accuracy","normal room temperature","poor technique"],"answer":3,"exp":"Beyond patient-related factors like mouth breathing, smoking, or recent hot/cold liquid intake, poor measurement technique itself is explicitly listed as a cause of false temperature readings. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which social history element is specifically relevant to concerning cardiac history?","choices":["Recent travel history","Occupation as a teacher","Smoking or smoke exposure","Dietary preference for vegetarianism"],"answer":2,"exp":"Smoking/smoke exposure is specifically listed as a relevant social history element for cardiac risk. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 19"},{"q":"During sinus transillumination, what technique and finding are used to assess sinus air-fluid status?","choices":["Transillumination is performed using the otoscope's light source placed directly into the external ear canal, rather than on the face at all","Keep the exam room brightly lit throughout the entire test and shine the light source directly into the nares only, never onto the face or forehead at all","Darken the room, place the light source on the maxilla or under the brow ridge, and look for a glow on the hard palate or forehead; decreased/absent glow suggests the sinus is not filled with air","A strong, bright glow observed on the hard palate or forehead during this particular test always indicates that active bacterial sinusitis is definitely present"],"answer":2,"exp":"This is the correct transillumination technique and interpretation described in the lecture. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 37"},{"q":"A perianal abscess, one of the listed anorectal abnormalities, typically presents on exam as...","choices":["complete absence of any visible or palpable finding","a symmetric bluish discoloration of the perianal skin with no tenderness","a painless, firm, non-mobile mass","a tender, fluctuant, erythematous swelling near the anus"],"answer":3,"exp":"A perianal abscess classically presents as a tender, fluctuant (compressible, suggesting fluid collection), erythematous (red/inflamed) swelling adjacent to the anus, reflecting a localized collection of infected fluid. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 11"},{"q":"Acute, severe scrotal pain accompanied by a high-riding, transversely-oriented testis and absent cremasteric reflex is most concerning for...","choices":["a routine finding needing no urgency at all","testicular torsion, a surgical emergency","a normal variant seen in some healthy men","Peyronie's disease, a penile curvature condition"],"answer":1,"exp":"This presentation is classic for testicular torsion — twisting of the spermatic cord cutting off blood supply — which is a surgical emergency requiring immediate evaluation to preserve testicular viability. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"To identify the upper border of liver dullness in the midclavicular line, the examiner starts at the nipple line and percusses...","choices":["only over the left side of the chest and abdomen","downward, from lung resonance toward liver dullness","side to side across the abdomen at each rib level","upward, from the umbilicus toward the nipple line"],"answer":1,"exp":"The upper border of liver dullness is found by starting at the nipple line (over resonant lung tissue) and percussing downward until the note changes from resonance to dullness, marking the upper liver border. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 26"},{"q":"Whispered pectoriloquy is assessed by having the patient whisper '1-2-3' or 'ninety-nine'; a positive finding is when the whispered words are heard...","choices":["exactly as faint and muffled as normal, unchanged","only as a nasal, goat-bleating 'A' sound instead","loudly and with clearer articulation than expected","not at all audible, not even faintly through the stethoscope"],"answer":2,"exp":"In whispered pectoriloquy, the normally muffled whispered words are instead heard loudly and clearly through the stethoscope, an extreme form of increased voice transmission associated with consolidation. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 52"},{"q":"When screening for concerning mole changes in the history, the examiner specifically asks about changes in...","choices":["nothing; mole changes are not screened for","size, shape, color, or sensation","only the patient's age","only the mole's exact anatomic location, never other features"],"answer":1,"exp":"The history specifically screens for moles that have changed in size, shape, color, or sensation, key features that can raise concern for malignant transformation. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 13"},{"q":"On inspection of the eyelids, the examiner screens for erythema, edema, lesions, and...","choices":["color vision","visual acuity","pupillary reaction","adequate closure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Eyelid inspection specifically includes checking for erythema, edema, lesions, and adequate closure, which is important for assessing corneal protection. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 22"},{"q":"Before palpating the carotid pulses, the examiner should always...","choices":["palpate them vigorously with firm pressure first","auscultate the carotid pulses first","palpate both carotids simultaneously with firm pressure","skip auscultation entirely for carotid pulses"],"answer":1,"exp":"The carotid pulses should always be auscultated (listening for a bruit) before palpating them, since vigorous palpation of a carotid artery with significant stenosis could theoretically dislodge plaque or, in a sensitive patient, trigger a vagal response. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 33"},{"q":"For the cardiac examination, how should the patient be positioned?","choices":["Sitting fully upright at 90 degrees","Supine with the head elevated 30 degrees","Flat supine with zero head elevation","Prone with the head turned laterally"],"answer":1,"exp":"The patient is placed supine with the head of the bed elevated 30 degrees for the cardiac exam. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 22"},{"q":"Before beginning the oral cavity exam, what should the examiner ask the patient to do?","choices":["Apply topical anesthetic to the oral mucosa","Refrain from speaking throughout the exam","Rinse with mouthwash immediately before the exam","Remove dentures or dental appliances"],"answer":3,"exp":"Dentures/dental appliances should be removed before inspection to allow full visualization of the oral mucosa and gums. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 49"},{"q":"A prostate that feels stony-hard with an irregular nodule on DRE (digital rectal examination) is most concerning for...","choices":["normal aging changes only","malignancy","a full bladder","a normal finding requiring no further workup"],"answer":1,"exp":"A stony-hard, irregular, nodular prostate on DRE (digital rectal examination) is the classic finding raising concern for prostate malignancy, in contrast to the smooth, symmetric, rubbery consistency of a normal prostate. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"Palpating each testis during the exam, the examiner should assess for...","choices":["size, shape, consistency, and any masses or tenderness","chest wall expansion and respiratory symmetry","blood pressure and resting pulse rate","bowel sounds heard in all four abdominal quadrants"],"answer":0,"exp":"Systematic testicular palpation assesses size, shape, consistency (should be smooth and firm, not nodular or stony), and screens for any masses or areas of tenderness. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"Percussion for the spleen along the left costal margin (Traube's space) should normally reveal a change from tympany to dullness occurring...","choices":["it should remain tympanic everywhere, with no area of dullness","between the anterior axillary line and mid-axillary line","only at the midclavicular line","only at the midline of the abdomen"],"answer":1,"exp":"In a normal-sized spleen, the percussion note changes from tympany to dullness within the expected zone between the anterior axillary line and mid-axillary line; dullness extending beyond this zone suggests splenomegaly. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 29"},{"q":"To assess diaphragmatic excursion posteriorly, the examiner first asks the patient to exhale and hold it while percussing down the scapular line until the sound changes from resonant to dull, marking that spot, then...","choices":["moves directly to auscultation of the lung bases instead, entirely skipping this second percussion step","repeats the identical exhale-and-hold maneuver a second time at the exact same previously marked percussion level","asks the patient to take a deep breath and hold it, continuing to percuss down to the new level where the sound becomes dull, marking that spot too","immediately measures the total excursion distance without ever marking a second reference spot on the back"],"answer":2,"exp":"After marking the level at full exhalation, the examiner has the patient take a few normal breaths, then take a deep breath and hold it, continuing percussion downward to mark the new resonant-to-dull transition point — the distance between the two marks is the diaphragmatic excursion. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 43"},{"q":"The recommended approach to opening the skin/hair/nail history is to...","choices":["only ask about a single narrow topic, such as current medications, right away first","start with a closed yes/no question asking about one specific suspected diagnosis first","skip the history-taking step entirely and proceed directly to the physical exam itself","start with an open-ended question, such as 'tell me what has been going on with your skin/hair/nails'"],"answer":3,"exp":"The recommended approach begins with an open-ended question or statement, allowing the patient to describe their concerns in their own words before the examiner asks more specific follow-up questions. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 13"},{"q":"To inspect the conjunctiva and sclera, the examiner gently pulls the lower lids down and asks the patient to look...","choices":["up, and then left and right","backward, which is not anatomically possible","straight ahead only, with eyes closed","down only, with no other direction needed"],"answer":0,"exp":"To fully inspect the conjunctiva and sclera, the examiner gently pulls the lower lids down while the patient looks up, then has the patient look left and right, spreading the lids with thumb and index finger for full view if necessary. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 23"},{"q":"The listed pulse locations to be familiar with include carotid, brachial, radial, apical, femoral, popliteal, dorsalis pedis, and...","choices":["no pulse locations are specified","posterior tibialis","temporal pulse only, with no others in the lower extremity","only the radial pulse is ever assessed"],"answer":1,"exp":"The full listed set of pulse locations includes carotid, brachial, radial, apical, femoral, popliteal, dorsalis pedis, and posterior tibialis pulses. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 32"},{"q":"If you are right-handed, where should you stand for best access to the heart during the cardiac exam?","choices":["At the foot of the bed","On the patient's right side","On the patient's left side","Behind the patient's head"],"answer":1,"exp":"A right-handed examiner should stand on the patient's right side for best access. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 22"},{"q":"When inspecting the buccal mucosa, which duct opening should specifically be identified?","choices":["The thoracic duct","Stensen's duct (parotid)","The nasolacrimal duct","Wharton's duct (submandibular)"],"answer":1,"exp":"Stensen's duct, the opening of the parotid gland, is specifically identified during buccal mucosa inspection. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 49"},{"q":"Wearing gloves throughout the anorectal exam serves primarily to...","choices":["warm the examiner's hands prior to any patient contact at all","make additional lubrication entirely unnecessary throughout the exam","protect both patient and examiner from contamination/infection","substantially improve the examiner's fine tactile sensitivity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Standard precautions require gloves for any exam involving contact with mucous membranes or body fluids, protecting both the patient and the examiner from potential infection transmission. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"Asking about penile discharge or lesions as part of the GU (genitourinary) history can help identify conditions such as...","choices":["only benign prostatic hyperplasia","sexually transmitted infections","hearing loss","only prostate cancer"],"answer":1,"exp":"Penile discharge or lesions are classic presenting symptoms of several sexually transmitted infections, making this a key screening question in the male GU (genitourinary) history. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"The splenic percussion sign is performed by percussing the left anterior axillary line in the lowest intercostal space and asking the patient to take a deep breath. A normal (negative) result shows...","choices":["a change from tympany to dullness during inhalation","no change in the percussion note during inhalation","a loud bruit heard instead of a percussion note","complete absence of any percussion note"],"answer":1,"exp":"In a normal (negative) splenic percussion sign, the percussion note in this area does not change from tympany to dullness during inhalation; a change to dullness with inspiration (positive sign) suggests splenic enlargement. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 29"},{"q":"Normal diaphragmatic excursion measures approximately how much bilaterally in adults, potentially up to 7-8 cm in well-conditioned individuals?","choices":["1 to 2 cm, with little variation across fitness levels","3 to 5 cm (up to 5.5 cm per the summary slide)","6 to 8 cm, expected even in an average, untrained adult","9 to 11 cm, typical bilaterally in most healthy adults"],"answer":1,"exp":"Normal diaphragmatic excursion is approximately 3 to 5 cm (summarized elsewhere as up to 5.5 cm) in typical adults, and can be as much as 7-8 cm in well-conditioned individuals with greater respiratory reserve. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 44"},{"q":"Past medical history relevant to the skin/hair/nail exam includes chronic illness with skin manifestations, surgical procedures, immunosuppression, pregnancies, psychiatric disease, and...","choices":["only a history of dental and orthodontic procedures","a history of blistering sunburns or radiation exposure","nothing beyond a history of chronic illness alone","only a history of prior visual acuity screening tests"],"answer":1,"exp":"The past medical history checklist includes a history of blistering sunburns and radiation exposure, both relevant risk factors for skin conditions including skin cancer. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 14"},{"q":"When inspecting the cornea, the examiner uses oblique lighting specifically to note...","choices":["visual acuity","pupil size only","color vision","any opacities"],"answer":3,"exp":"Oblique (angled) lighting on the cornea helps the examiner detect subtle opacities that might not be visible with direct frontal lighting. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 24"},{"q":"The normal adult heart rate range, assessed via the radial pulse, is approximately...","choices":["60-100 beats per minute","20-40 beats per minute","150-200 beats per minute","there is no defined normal range"],"answer":0,"exp":"The normal adult heart rate range, though it varies with age, fitness level, and medications, is approximately 60-100 beats per minute. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 39"},{"q":"When auscultating for carotid bruits, why must you ask the patient to stop breathing for about 10 seconds?","choices":["To allow simultaneous bimanual palpation of both carotid pulses","To transiently lower the patient's blood pressure before listening","To transiently increase blood flow velocity through the carotid","To eliminate breath sounds that could obscure a subtle bruit"],"answer":3,"exp":"Holding breath removes tracheal/breath sound interference so a faint carotid bruit can be heard clearly. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 24"},{"q":"When inspecting the floor of the mouth, which duct opening should be identified?","choices":["The lacrimal punctum opening","Wharton's duct (submandibular)","Stensen's duct (parotid)","The eustachian tube opening"],"answer":1,"exp":"Wharton's duct, the submandibular gland's duct opening, is identified during floor-of-mouth inspection. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 50"},{"q":"Anal warts (condyloma acuminata) typically appear on inspection as...","choices":["small, flesh-colored, cauliflower-like papules around the anus","a single deep linear tear located in the posterior midline","a smooth, fluctuant, tender swelling located near the anus","complete and diffuse loss of perianal skin pigmentation entirely"],"answer":0,"exp":"Anal warts (condyloma acuminata), caused by HPV (human papillomavirus), classically appear as small, flesh-colored, cauliflower-like (verrucous) papules or clusters around the anal region. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 11"},{"q":"Wearing gloves during the male GU (genitourinary) exam is a standard requirement primarily because...","choices":["it is entirely optional per standard technique, left up to the examiner's personal preference","it eliminates the entirely separate need to explain the exam process to the patient beforehand","it meaningfully improves the examiner's fine tactile sensitivity during genital palpation exams","it protects both patient and examiner per standard precautions when examining genitalia/mucous membranes"],"answer":3,"exp":"As with any exam involving contact with genitalia or potential mucosal/body fluid exposure, gloves are required under standard precautions to protect both the patient and the examiner. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"Light palpation of the abdomen is performed to a depth of approximately...","choices":["less than 1 cm","3 to 4 cm","5 to 10 cm","there is no defined depth for light palpation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Light palpation is a superficial technique, pressing to a depth of less than 1 cm, used to identify tenderness, muscular resistance, and superficial organs/masses before deeper palpation. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 35"},{"q":"The level of the diaphragm on percussion is often normally higher on the right side compared with the left because of the...","choices":["stomach","liver","spleen","heart"],"answer":1,"exp":"The liver, positioned beneath the right hemidiaphragm, pushes the diaphragm slightly higher on the right side compared with the left in many individuals, a normal asymmetry to keep in mind when assessing diaphragmatic level. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 44"},{"q":"Family history relevant to the skin/hair/nail exam includes skin cancers, atopic conditions (eczema, asthma), psoriasis, hair loss, and...","choices":["cardiac valve disease exclusively","nothing; family history is not relevant here","only kidney disease","pigmentation patterns"],"answer":3,"exp":"The family history checklist includes skin cancers, atopic conditions (eczema, asthma, allergies), psoriasis, hair loss, and pigmentation patterns. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 15"},{"q":"Pupil size should be measured in dim light; a pupil is considered small if it is under approximately...","choices":["0.5 mm","10 mm","3 mm","there is no defined threshold for a small pupil"],"answer":2,"exp":"A pupil measured in dim light is considered small if it is under approximately 3 mm, and large if it exceeds approximately 5 mm. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 24"},{"q":"When assessing a regular pulse, the standard technique is to count for 30 seconds and multiply by 2; for an irregular pulse, the examiner should instead...","choices":["count for a full minute","not count the pulse at all if irregular","use the same 30-second method as for a regular pulse","count for only 10 seconds and multiply by 6"],"answer":0,"exp":"Because an irregular pulse may not be evenly distributed across a 30-second sample, a full minute of counting is used instead of the 30-second-times-2 shortcut used for regular pulses, giving a more accurate rate. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 39"},{"q":"Where should the stethoscope be placed to auscultate for carotid bruits?","choices":["Directly over the sternal notch at the base of the neck","Near the upper end of the thyroid cartilage, below the angle of the jaw","Over the midclavicular line at the fifth intercostal space","At the angle of the mandible only, never below that level"],"answer":1,"exp":"This location overlies the carotid bifurcation and reduces confusion with a transmitted cardiac murmur. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 24"},{"q":"When assessing the soft palate and uvula, what specific maneuver is used?","choices":["Shine a light source directly into the external ear canal","Ask the patient to say \"ah\" and observe for symmetrical rise/fall","Ask the patient to hold their breath for a full ten seconds","Palpate the uvula directly with a single gloved index finger"],"answer":1,"exp":"Having the patient say \"ah\" allows observation of symmetric elevation of the soft palate/uvula, which also screens vagus (CN (cranial nerve) X) function. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 50"},{"q":"Adequate draping during the anorectal exam serves to...","choices":["completely and permanently prevent the ongoing need for a chaperone present during the exam","completely and permanently eliminate the separate need for adequate examination room lighting","preserve patient dignity/comfort while still allowing adequate exposure of the area being examined","completely and permanently replace the separate need for documented verbal patient consent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Proper draping balances patient dignity and comfort with the examiner's need for adequate visualization and access to the perianal area, exposing only what is necessary for the exam. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"An indirect inguinal hernia differs from a direct inguinal hernia primarily in its anatomic path, in that an indirect hernia...","choices":["passes through the internal inguinal ring, following the path of the spermatic cord, and can descend into the scrotum","always occurs exclusively in female patients, since the spermatic cord pathway doesn't exist in men at all","never reaches the scrotum, remaining confined entirely above the level of the inguinal ligament always","bulges directly through Hesselbach's triangle, medial to the inferior epigastric vessels, every single time"],"answer":0,"exp":"An indirect inguinal hernia protrudes through the internal inguinal ring and follows the same path as the spermatic cord through the inguinal canal, and can extend into the scrotum, whereas a direct hernia bulges through a weakness in the abdominal wall (Hesselbach's triangle) medial to the inferior epigastric vessels. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"During light palpation, the examiner's technique should involve...","choices":["pressing firmly downward, roughly 3-4 cm deep into the abdomen, using the whole flat hand in one motion","using only the isolated fingertips individually, never engaging the entire flat hand at once together","a vertical hand plane with the fingers spread widely apart, pressing straight downward with firm pressure","a horizontal hand plane, fingers together, using a light gentle dipping motion, barely raising the hand off the skin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Light palpation technique specifically calls for the hand held in a horizontal plane with fingers together, using a light, gentle dipping motion, barely lifting the hand off the skin between presses. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 35"},{"q":"Decreased diaphragmatic excursion on assessment can result from several possible causes, including atelectasis of the lower lobes, emphysema, pleural effusion, pain, or...","choices":["well-controlled asthma with normal spirometry values","abdominal changes such as tumors or extreme ascites","vigorous aerobic exercise training performed three times weekly","normal aging alone, without any underlying lung disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Beyond pulmonary causes (atelectasis, emphysema, pleural effusion) and pain limiting inspiratory effort, decreased diaphragmatic excursion can also result from abdominal processes that restrict diaphragmatic descent, such as abdominal tumors or extreme ascites. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 44"},{"q":"The four general physical examination techniques applied to the skin exam are inspection, palpation, percussion, and auscultation, though for skin the most heavily used are...","choices":["none of the four techniques apply to skin","percussion and auscultation exclusively","only auscultation","inspection and palpation"],"answer":3,"exp":"While all four general exam techniques are mentioned, the skin exam relies most heavily on inspection ('what do you see?') and palpation ('what do you feel?'), since percussion and auscultation have limited direct application to skin assessment itself. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 16"},{"q":"A pupil is considered large if it measures greater than approximately...","choices":["5 mm","there is no defined threshold for a large pupil","1 mm","20 mm"],"answer":0,"exp":"A pupil measured in dim light is considered large if it exceeds approximately 5 mm, with under approximately 3 mm being the small-pupil threshold. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 24"},{"q":"The normal adult respiratory rate is approximately...","choices":["there is no normal adult respiratory rate range","40-60 breaths per minute","12-20 breaths per minute","1-5 breaths per minute"],"answer":2,"exp":"Normal adult respiratory rate is approximately 12-20 breaths per minute; normal rates are considerably faster in infants and young children (for example, over 60/min at birth to 2 months) and gradually decrease toward the adult range through childhood. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which layer of an artery is where atherosclerosis characteristically forms?","choices":["Media","Vasa vasorum","Adventitia","Intima"],"answer":3,"exp":"The intima, the single continuous endothelial lining, is where atherosclerosis characteristically forms. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 32"},{"q":"When inspecting the tonsillar pillars and tonsils, which findings should be assessed?","choices":["Only symmetry of the uvula","Redness, swelling, exudate, ulceration, and hypertrophy","Only the presence of a gag reflex","Only tonsil size graded on a 0-4 scale with no other findings"],"answer":1,"exp":"These are the specific tonsillar inspection findings listed in the lecture. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 51"},{"q":"A patient reporting pain specifically during defecation, as part of the anorectal history, should prompt the examiner to particularly consider conditions such as...","choices":["hearing changes","a normal, unremarkable history requiring no further exam","anal fissure or thrombosed hemorrhoid","asymptomatic internal hemorrhoids only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Pain specifically with defecation is a hallmark symptom of conditions like an anal fissure or a thrombosed external hemorrhoid, both of which are painful, defecation-related anorectal conditions. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"Inspecting the urethral meatus as part of the genital exam allows the examiner to check for...","choices":["visual acuity and near/far pupillary accommodation reflex testing bilaterally","lung sounds heard bilaterally during a routine chest auscultation exam","discharge, lesions, or abnormal positioning of the opening (for example, hypospadias)","heart rate, rhythm regularity, and point of maximal cardiac impulse location"],"answer":2,"exp":"Inspecting the urethral meatus screens for discharge (which may suggest infection), lesions, and abnormal positioning of the opening such as hypospadias (urethral opening on the ventral penile shaft rather than the tip). — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"During both light and deep palpation, any area of known tenderness should be examined...","choices":["first, before any of the other abdominal areas","last, after all other areas have been assessed","using the same technique, regardless of the chosen order","only if the patient specifically requests it directly"],"answer":1,"exp":"To avoid causing pain that could make the patient guard and complicate the rest of the exam, the area of known/suspected tenderness should always be palpated last. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 35"},{"q":"When percussing the posterior chest, the examiner should proceed systematically, comparing side to side, at intervals of approximately...","choices":["only at a single fixed point, with no systematic movement","20 cm intervals","4 to 5 cm, moving from superior to inferior","from inferior to superior only, never the reverse"],"answer":2,"exp":"Posterior chest percussion is performed systematically, comparing side to side at each level, at roughly 4-5 cm intervals, proceeding from superior to inferior across the intercostal spaces. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 36"},{"q":"The 'integrated examination' approach to the skin, as distinguished from a dedicated full-body exam, involves...","choices":["never examining the skin at all, regardless of what other components of the physical exam are being performed","examining only the patient's face, regardless of what other exam components are simultaneously being performed","a completely separate, dedicated full-body exam performed only once, during the patient's initial intake visit","inspecting/palpating exposed skin that becomes accessible during other exam components (for example, back/chest during lung auscultation)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The integrated examination approach assesses skin opportunistically as it becomes exposed during other parts of the physical exam — for example, the back and chest during lung auscultation, or the abdomen during the abdominal exam — rather than as one dedicated, separate full-body skin exam. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 17"},{"q":"When testing pupillary reaction to light, shining a bright light obliquely into one eye and observing constriction in that same eye is called the...","choices":["corneal light reflection","consensual reaction","direct reaction","near reaction"],"answer":2,"exp":"The direct pupillary reaction is constriction observed in the same eye that is being directly illuminated, distinct from the consensual reaction, which is constriction observed in the opposite, non-illuminated eye. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 25"},{"q":"To obtain the most accurate respiratory rate, the recommended technique is to...","choices":["observe respirations while still palpating the radial pulse, so the patient is unaware they are being observed","openly ask the patient to breathe as fast and as deeply as possible while being timed","count breaths for only five seconds and then multiply that short count by twelve","announce clearly and directly to the patient that their respirations are now being counted"],"answer":0,"exp":"Because patients may unconsciously alter their breathing pattern if they know it's being observed, the recommended technique is to continue palpating the radial pulse (as if still counting heart rate) while actually observing respirations, keeping the patient unaware. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which arterial layer consists of smooth muscle with elastic properties that accommodate blood pressure and flow?","choices":["Endothelium alone","Intima","Adventitia","Media"],"answer":3,"exp":"The media contains smooth muscle with elastin, accommodating pressure and flow changes. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 32"},{"q":"When using a tongue blade to visualize the pharynx, what technique should be used?","choices":["Insert the tongue blade as far posteriorly as possible","Avoid touching the tongue entirely and rely on the patient opening wide","Press lightly only on the tip of the tongue","Press firmly on the midpoint of the tongue, without going too far back"],"answer":3,"exp":"Firm pressure at the tongue midpoint provides visualization while avoiding the far posterior tongue, which can trigger a strong gag reflex. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 51"}]};
